IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Children's Court of Western Australia Act 1988 (Western Australia) -- which court deals with a child charged with an
-- offence (ss 3, 19, 19A-19D, 30(1)); the limits on what the Court as constituted can sentence (ss 21, 22); a parent's
-- attendance (s 23); who may be in the courtroom and who learns the child's name afterwards (ss 31, 33); the duty to make
-- sure the child understands (s 34); publication of the child's identity and disclosure of the outcome (ss 35, 36, 36AA,
-- 36A); and the child's own routes to have an order looked at again (ss 28, 40).
--
-- SEAT: the child before the court (Jake, at 15). Every decision is phrased as that person would ask it: "Am I a child
-- for this charge?", "Which court deals with my charge?", "May I choose a trial on indictment?", "Who may stay in the
-- courtroom?", "May a report identifying me be published?", "May someone tell others of my conviction?", "Can I ask the
-- President to reconsider my sentence?". Where the Act hands a judgment to the Court (s 19B(1)(b)(ii) "decides that it is
-- to be tried on indictment", s 19C(1)(b) "of the opinion that it is appropriate", s 19D "may ... order the transfer",
-- s 23(1) "valid reason to excuse attendance", s 31(1) "may order that any persons shall be excluded", s 31(2) "satisfies
-- the Court", s 36A "after considering the public interest"), the answer is conditional on that judgment, asked as a fact.
--
-- Provisions covered: s 3 ("child", "CEO (young offenders)"), s 4(2) (as a comment), s 19(1), (1a) (as a comment), (2),
-- (2AA), (2AB), (4), s 19A, s 19B(1)-(4), s 19C(1), s 19D, s 21(2), (4), s 22(2), (3), s 23(1a), (1), s 28(1), (2)(c),
-- s 30(1), s 31(1), (2), s 33(2), s 34(1), (3), s 35(1)-(4), s 36(1), (2), s 36AA(1), s 36A(1), (3), s 40(1), (2)(a), (5).
--
-- Edition: compilation from legislation.wa.gov.au (mrdoc 49136, retrieved 7 September 2026), as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49136.txt. The deposit names no compilation number. Its latest amendment shown
-- in the text is No. 35 of 2024 s 93 (inserting s 40A). No regulations set a number used here; the numbers (the age 18,
-- 12 months, 3 months, 6 months, one month, $10 000) are the Act's own.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: Part 2 (establishment, constitution, judges, magistrates, registrars: machinery); s 19(9) and s 19E;
-- s 19B(5), (6) (statements in evidence); s 19C(3), (4); s 20 (non-criminal jurisdiction: care and protection, school
-- attendance, restraining orders, prohibited behaviour orders); s 21(4A), (4B), (5), (6); s 27; s 29 (contempt); s 30(2)
-- (a decision made in the belief the person was under 18); s 36A(2), (4), (5); s 40A; ss 41-43 (appeals, which run under
-- the Criminal Appeals Act 2004); Part 7 (records, regulations, fees). The Young Offenders Act 1994 Part 5 Division 2 and
-- the Sentencing Act 1995 provisions that s 19(1a) makes s 19(1) subject to are other Acts (the first is encoded in the
-- Young Offenders Act 1994 baseline). Whether an offence is one an adult must be tried for on indictment, or is an
-- indictable offence that may be tried summarily, is set by The Criminal Code s 5 and other written laws: an INPUT.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the
-- individual to whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Children's Court of Western Australia Act 1988 (WA)`

§§ `numbers and helpers`

-- s 3 "child" (a): "any boy or girl under the age of 18 years".
`the age of adulthood` MEANS 18

-- s 21(2)(a): "a term of detention longer than 12 months".
`the most detention a magistrate may impose, in months` MEANS 12

-- s 21(2)(b): "a term of imprisonment longer than 3 months" (offender under 18 at sentencing).
`the most imprisonment a magistrate may impose on someone under 18, in months` MEANS 3

-- s 21(2)(c): "a term of imprisonment longer than 6 months" (offender 18 or over at sentencing).
`the most imprisonment a magistrate may impose on someone 18 or over, in months` MEANS 6

-- ss 35(4)(b), 36(2)(b): "a fine of $10 000 or imprisonment for 12 months".
`the publication fine` MEANS 10000

GIVEN `born on` IS A DATE
      `on`      IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`age in completed years` MEANS
    IF     DATE_MONTH `on` GREATER THAN DATE_MONTH `born on`
        OR (DATE_MONTH `on` EQUALS DATE_MONTH `born on` AND DATE_DAY `on` AT LEAST DATE_DAY `born on`)
    THEN DATE_YEAR `on` MINUS DATE_YEAR `born on`
    ELSE DATE_YEAR `on` MINUS DATE_YEAR `born on` MINUS 1

-- The same day of the next calendar month. (A day that does not exist in the next month, such as 31 January, is left to
-- the daydate library's rolling and is not relied on here.)
GIVEN `d` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`one month after` MEANS
    IF DATE_MONTH `d` EQUALS 12
    THEN Date (DATE_DAY `d`) 1 (DATE_YEAR `d` PLUS 1)
    ELSE Date (DATE_DAY `d`) (DATE_MONTH `d` PLUS 1) (DATE_YEAR `d`)

§§ `3, 19 -- am I a child for this charge`

-- s 19(1): "The Court has exclusive jurisdiction to hear and determine a charge of an offence alleged to have been committed
-- by a child." s 19(2): "Notwithstanding that a person has attained the age of 18 years, the jurisdiction of the Court
-- extends ... to proceedings in respect of an offence committed, or allegedly committed, by the person before attaining the
-- age of 18 years." s 19(2AA): "Despite any uncertainty as to the age of the person charged at the time the offence is
-- alleged to have been committed, the Court has jurisdiction ... if the charge alleges that the offence was committed by a
-- person who might have been a child." s 19(2AB): it keeps that jurisdiction "despite any evidence ... that the person had
-- reached 18 years of age". s 3 "child" includes "(c) a person dealt with under section 19(2), (2AA) or (2AB)".
-- So the test is the age on the day of the offence, as the Young Offenders Act 1994 s 4 has it for "young person".
-- s 19(1a): s 19(1) is "subject to ... the Young Offenders Act 1994 Part 5 Division 2" (referral to a juvenile justice team)
-- "and the Sentencing Act 1995 sections 78, 84D, 128 and 132" -- other Acts, not encoded here.
DECLARE `My charge` HAS
    `my date of birth`                                                   IS A DATE
    `the date of the offence`                                            IS A DATE     -- s 19(1), (2)
    `my age at the offence is uncertain and the charge says I might have been a child` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 19(2AA), (2AB)
    `the kind of offence`                                                IS A `Kind of offence`
    `the date the Court deals with the charge`                           IS A DATE     -- s 19D: "has attained the age of 18"
    `the Court decides my either-way charge is to be tried on indictment` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 19B(1)(b)(ii): a judgment
    `I elect to be tried on indictment`                                  IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 19B(1)
    `an adult charged with the same offence is to be tried on indictment and the Court thinks a joint trial appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 19C(1): a judgment
    `the Court orders the charge transferred to the Magistrates Court under s 19D` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 19D: a judgment

-- s 19B(1): "(a) the offence is such that, if an adult were charged with it, it must be tried on indictment; or (b) ... if an
-- adult were charged with it, it could, by virtue of section 5 of The Criminal Code, or another written law, be tried either
-- on indictment or summarily". Which kind an offence is, is set by The Criminal Code and other written laws (an input).
DECLARE `Kind of offence` IS ONE OF
    `a simple offence`
    `an indictable offence that may be tried summarily`
    `an indictable offence an adult must be tried for on indictment`

@export Am I a child for the purposes of this charge in the Children's Court (Children's Court of Western Australia Act 1988 (WA) ss 3, 19)?
GIVEN `my date of birth` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth
      `the date of the offence` IS A DATE @desc The date the offence was (or is said to have been) committed
      `my age is uncertain and the charge says I might have been a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your age at the time is uncertain and the charge alleges you might have been under 18
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I a child for this charge` IF
        `age in completed years` `my date of birth` `the date of the offence` LESS THAN `the age of adulthood`
    OR  `my age is uncertain and the charge says I might have been a child`

GIVEN `the charge` IS A `My charge`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`I am a child for it` MEANS
    `Am I a child for this charge` (`the charge`'s `my date of birth`) (`the charge`'s `the date of the offence`)
                                   (`the charge`'s `my age at the offence is uncertain and the charge says I might have been a child`)

GIVEN `the charge` IS A `My charge`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`it is indictable` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the charge`'s `the kind of offence`
    WHEN `a simple offence` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `19B -- may I choose a trial on indictment`

-- s 19B(1): if a child is charged with an indictable offence that an adult must be tried for on indictment, or one that may
-- be tried either way and "the Court, having complied with section 40(2) of the Criminal Procedure Act 2004, decides that it
-- is to be tried on indictment", "the child may elect to be tried on indictment by the Supreme Court or the District Court
-- (as the case requires), and the Court shall so inform the child." s 19A: the same for a person charged with an indictable
-- offence "alleged to have been committed when that person was a child".
-- The election is the child's: for an either-way offence it opens only after the Court has decided on indictment.
@export May I choose to be tried on indictment by the Supreme Court or District Court (Children's Court of Western Australia Act 1988 (WA) s 19B(1))?
GIVEN `the charge` IS A `My charge` @desc The charge against you and what the Court has decided about it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I elect to be tried on indictment` IF
        `I am a child for it` `the charge`
    AND (    CONSIDER `the charge`'s `the kind of offence`
             WHEN `an indictable offence an adult must be tried for on indictment` THEN TRUE
             WHEN `an indictable offence that may be tried summarily` THEN `the charge`'s `the Court decides my either-way charge is to be tried on indictment`
             OTHERWISE FALSE)

-- s 19B(1): "the Court shall so inform the child".
@export Must the Children's Court tell me that I may choose a trial on indictment (s 19B(1))?
GIVEN `the charge` IS A `My charge` @desc The charge against you and what the Court has decided about it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the Court tell me I may elect a trial on indictment` IF
    `May I elect to be tried on indictment` `the charge`

-- s 19B(4)(c): if the child does not elect, the Court hears the charge "as if the prosecution notice were an indictment, and
-- the hearing were a trial on indictment ... but the child is not thereby entitled to have any issue tried by a jury". (d):
-- if convicted, the child is "taken to have been convicted on indictment".
-- A jury is had only by electing (s 19B(3) sends an elected charge up "as if the charge were one that must be tried on
-- indictment"; INFERENCE: that trial is with a jury under the Criminal Procedure Act 2004, which is not encoded).
@export Can I have a jury if I stay in the Children's Court for an indictable charge (s 19B(4)(c))?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I have a jury in the Children's Court` IS FALSE

§§ `19, 19B, 19C, 19D, 30 -- which court deals with my charge`

-- s 19(1): exclusive jurisdiction for a child. s 19B(2): a child who cannot elect has the charge heard "summarily". s 19B(3):
-- an elected charge goes to "Part 3 Division 4 of the Criminal Procedure Act 2004 as if the charge were one that must be tried
-- on indictment" (committal to the Supreme Court or District Court). s 19C(1): "Notwithstanding section 19B", if an adult is
-- charged with the same indictable offence, "is to be tried on indictment", and the Court "is for any reason of the opinion
-- that it is appropriate that the child be dealt with on indictment jointly with the adult", the charge goes up as one that
-- must be tried on indictment. s 19D: "Notwithstanding section 19B", where a person charged with an indictable offence
-- alleged to have been committed under 18 "has attained the age of 18 years", the Court "may ... order the transfer of the
-- prosecution notice to the Magistrates Court". s 30(1): if the Court "is of the opinion that it does not have
-- jurisdiction", it "may order the transfer of the matter to a court of competent jurisdiction".
-- s 19B(4) also lets a child who does not elect be heard by the Children's Court as if on indictment, without a jury.
-- Order of the checks: no jurisdiction first; then s 19C and s 19D, which apply "notwithstanding section 19B"; then s 19B.
-- s 19C and s 19D are only for indictable offences (s 19A). s 19D's "has attained the age of 18" is read at the date the
-- Court deals with the charge (INFERENCE from "has attained").
DECLARE `Court for my charge` IS ONE OF
    `the Children's Court`
    `the Supreme Court or District Court, on indictment, because I elected`
    `the Supreme Court or District Court, on indictment, jointly with an adult`
    `the Magistrates Court, on transfer because I am now 18`
    `an adult court: I was not a child at the offence`

@export Which court deals with my charge (Children's Court of Western Australia Act 1988 (WA) ss 19, 19B, 19C, 19D, 30)?
GIVEN `the charge` IS A `My charge` @desc The charge against you and what the Court has decided about it
GIVETH A `Court for my charge`
DECIDE `Which court deals with my charge` IS
    IF (NOT `I am a child for it` `the charge`)
    THEN `an adult court: I was not a child at the offence`
    ELSE IF `it is indictable` `the charge` AND `the charge`'s `an adult charged with the same offence is to be tried on indictment and the Court thinks a joint trial appropriate`
         THEN `the Supreme Court or District Court, on indictment, jointly with an adult`
         ELSE IF     `it is indictable` `the charge`
                 AND `age in completed years` (`the charge`'s `my date of birth`) (`the charge`'s `the date the Court deals with the charge`) AT LEAST `the age of adulthood`
                 AND `the charge`'s `the Court orders the charge transferred to the Magistrates Court under s 19D`
              THEN `the Magistrates Court, on transfer because I am now 18`
              ELSE IF `May I elect to be tried on indictment` `the charge` AND `the charge`'s `I elect to be tried on indictment`
                   THEN `the Supreme Court or District Court, on indictment, because I elected`
                   ELSE `the Children's Court`

-- s 19D lists what the Court may have regard to: "(a) the seriousness of the offence; or (b) the existence of an adult
-- co-offender; or (c) the effluxion of time since the offence; or (d) the fact that the person is charged before the
-- Magistrates Court with other offences; or (e) any other good cause". The transfer itself is the Court's judgment (an input).
@export Could the Children's Court send my charge to the Magistrates Court because I have turned 18 (s 19D)?
GIVEN `the charge` IS A `My charge` @desc The charge against you
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Could my charge be transferred because I have turned 18` IF
        `I am a child for it` `the charge`
    AND `it is indictable` `the charge`
    AND `age in completed years` (`the charge`'s `my date of birth`) (`the charge`'s `the date the Court deals with the charge`) AT LEAST `the age of adulthood`

-- s 19(4): "Where it is provided by any other written law that a person instead of being prosecuted for a simple offence in a
-- court may elect to suffer a prescribed monetary penalty in respect of that offence, then, notwithstanding anything in this
-- Act, a child may so elect."
@export May I choose to pay a prescribed penalty instead of being prosecuted (s 19(4))?
GIVEN `another written law offers that choice for this simple offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another law lets a person pay a set penalty instead of being prosecuted for this simple offence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I elect to pay a prescribed penalty instead` IF
    `another written law offers that choice for this simple offence`

§§ `21, 22 -- how much the Court as constituted can give me`

-- s 21(2): "The Court when constituted by or so as to include a magistrate cannot -- (a) sentence an offender to a term of
-- detention longer than 12 months; or (b) sentence an offender who at the time of being sentenced is under 18 years of age to
-- a term of imprisonment longer than 3 months; or (c) sentence an offender who at the time of being sentenced has reached 18
-- years of age to a term of imprisonment longer than 6 months, for one offence, or as the aggregate of the sentences imposed
-- on the one occasion for more than one offence." s 21(4): "When constituted by JPs only, the Court may not -- (a) sentence
-- a child to detention or to be imprisoned". s 22(2): a magistrate the President directs under s 22 has "extended powers of
-- sentencing as if that magistrate were a judge". This Act sets no cap for a judge (the caps are in other Acts, not encoded).
DECLARE `Who sits` IS ONE OF
    `a judge`
    `a magistrate`
    `a magistrate given extended powers under s 22`
    `justices of the peace only`

DECLARE `Kind of custody` IS ONE OF
    `detention`
    `imprisonment`

DECLARE `Custody cap` IS ONE OF
    `no cap in this Act`
    `none may be imposed`
    `at most` HAS `months` IS A NUMBER

@export What is the most detention or imprisonment the Children's Court, as constituted, can give me (ss 21, 22)?
GIVEN `who sits` IS A `Who sits` @desc Who constitutes the Court at your sentencing
      `the kind` IS A `Kind of custody` @desc Detention or imprisonment
      `my date of birth` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth
      `the date of sentencing` IS A DATE @desc The day you are sentenced
GIVETH A `Custody cap`
DECIDE `What is the most custody the Court can give me` IS
    CONSIDER `who sits`
    WHEN `a judge` THEN `no cap in this Act`
    WHEN `a magistrate given extended powers under s 22` THEN `no cap in this Act`
    WHEN `justices of the peace only` THEN `none may be imposed`
    WHEN `a magistrate` THEN
        CONSIDER `the kind`
        WHEN `detention` THEN `at most` `the most detention a magistrate may impose, in months`
        WHEN `imprisonment` THEN
            IF `age in completed years` `my date of birth` `the date of sentencing` LESS THAN `the age of adulthood`
            THEN `at most` `the most imprisonment a magistrate may impose on someone under 18, in months`
            ELSE `at most` `the most imprisonment a magistrate may impose on someone 18 or over, in months`

-- s 22(3): "the magistrate must when the trial begins inform the person charged of the extended powers conferred on the
-- magistrate under this section."
@export Must the magistrate tell me at the start that they have a judge's sentencing powers (s 22(3))?
GIVEN `the President directed the magistrate under s 22` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the President gave the magistrate extended powers for your charge
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the magistrate tell me of extended powers` IF
    `the President directed the magistrate under s 22`

§§ `23 -- must my parent or guardian be made to come`

-- s 23(1a): "This section applies to proceedings other than those to which section 45 of the Young Offenders Act 1994
-- applies." (Young Offenders Act 1994 s 45(1), read in its own deposit, applies "In proceedings against a young person for
-- an offence", and requires a responsible adult in much the same terms.) s 23(1): "the Court shall inquire in to the reason
-- if no parent or guardian of the child is present and, unless the Court considers that -- (a) there is a valid reason to
-- excuse attendance; or (b) it is not reasonable to delay proceedings ... the Court shall, by order served on a parent or
-- guardian ... require such parent or guardian to attend during all stages of the proceedings".
-- So for Jake's criminal charge the duty is the Young Offenders Act's, not this Act's.
@export Must the Children's Court order my parent or guardian to attend, under this Act (s 23)?
GIVEN `these are proceedings against me for an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are charged with an offence (then the Young Offenders Act 1994 s 45 applies instead)
      `a parent or guardian is present` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a parent or guardian of yours is in court
      `the Court finds a valid reason to excuse attendance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court considers there is a valid reason to excuse a parent's attendance
      `the Court finds it unreasonable to delay for a parent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court considers it not reasonable to delay for a parent or guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the Court order my parent or guardian to attend under this Act` IF
        (NOT `these are proceedings against me for an offence`)
    AND (NOT `a parent or guardian is present`)
    AND (NOT `the Court finds a valid reason to excuse attendance`)
    AND (NOT `the Court finds it unreasonable to delay for a parent`)

§§ `31, 33, 34 -- who may be present, and what I must understand`

-- s 31(1): "At any hearing or trial relating to a charge against, or any application concerning, a child ... the Court may
-- order that any persons shall be excluded from the courtroom or place of hearing." s 33(2): a "prescribed officer" (the
-- CEO (young offenders) or an authorised officer of the young offenders agency; or the child protection CEO or an authorised
-- officer) "may be present at the hearing ... and, if present, is entitled to examine and cross-examine witnesses and to be
-- heard concerning the remand, punishment or disposal of the child."
-- The Act says nothing on which of s 31 and s 33 prevails. Reading s 33's specific entitlement as surviving a general s 31
-- exclusion order is an INFERENCE. The Act gives no one else a right to stay once excluded.
DECLARE `Person in the courtroom` IS ONE OF
    `a prescribed officer of the young offenders agency or the child protection department`
    `my parent or guardian`
    `the victim`
    `a reporter`
    `a member of the public`

@export May this person stay in the courtroom at my hearing (Children's Court of Western Australia Act 1988 (WA) ss 31, 33)?
GIVEN `the person` IS A `Person in the courtroom` @desc Who wants to be in the courtroom
      `the Court ordered this person excluded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered under s 31 that this person (or persons including them) leave
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May this person stay in the courtroom` IF
    CONSIDER `the person`
    WHEN `a prescribed officer of the young offenders agency or the child protection department` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE (NOT `the Court ordered this person excluded`)

-- s 31(2): "Where the Court has made an order under subsection (1), it shall after the conclusion of the hearing, make
-- available to any person who satisfies the Court that that person has suffered loss by reason of an offence committed by the
-- child or has other good cause, the name, age, and address of the child and the particulars of the offence with which the
-- child was charged."
@export Must the Court give this person my name, age, address and the charge after a closed hearing (s 31(2))?
GIVEN `the Court made an exclusion order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court excluded people from your hearing under s 31(1)
      `the hearing has concluded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the hearing is over
      `the person satisfied the Court of loss from the offence or other good cause` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person showed the Court they lost something because of the offence, or had other good cause
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the Court give this person my name and address` IF
        `the Court made an exclusion order`
    AND `the hearing has concluded`
    AND `the person satisfied the Court of loss from the offence or other good cause`

-- s 34(1): "In any proceedings before the Court, the Court must satisfy itself that the child who is the subject of the
-- proceedings understands the nature of those proceedings." (3): "No order or adjudication of the Court is defective on the
-- ground of failure to comply with this section if the Court has substantially complied".
@export Must the Children's Court make sure I understand what the proceedings are about (s 34(1))?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the Court make sure I understand the proceedings` IS TRUE

-- s 34(3) saves an order only where the Court "substantially complied". It does not say an order is defective otherwise.
@export Is the order against me saved, though the Court's explanation fell short (s 34(3))?
GIVEN `the Court substantially complied with s 34` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court substantially did what s 34 requires
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my order saved despite a short explanation` IF
    `the Court substantially complied with s 34`

§§ `35, 36A -- may I be identified in a report`

-- s 35(1): "Except where done in accordance with an order made under section 36A or in accordance with the Prohibited
-- Behaviour Orders Act 2010 section 34, a person shall not publish ... or broadcast ... a report of any proceedings in the
-- Court, or in any other court on appeal from the Court, containing any particulars or other matter likely to lead to the
-- identification of a child who is concerned in those proceedings -- (a) as a person against whom the proceedings are taken".
-- s 35(2): in criminal proceedings in the Supreme Court or the District Court to which (1) does not apply, that court "may,
-- after considering the public interest and the interests of the child ... order that no person shall publish" such a report.
-- So: in the Children's Court and on appeal from it, the ban is automatic; in the Supreme or District Court on indictment,
-- there is a ban only if that court orders one. (s 35(3), the ban for child victims of Chapter XXII or XXXI offences, is
-- about the child as victim, not as accused: not encoded.)
DECLARE `Where I am tried` IS ONE OF
    `in the Children's Court`
    `in a court hearing an appeal from the Children's Court`
    `in the Supreme Court or District Court, not on appeal from the Children's Court`

@export May a report that identifies me as the accused be published (Children's Court of Western Australia Act 1988 (WA) ss 35, 36A)?
GIVEN `where` IS A `Where I am tried` @desc The court whose proceedings the report is about
      `the Supreme Court allowed it under s 36A or the PBO Act s 34 permits it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Supreme Court order under s 36A (or the Prohibited Behaviour Orders Act 2010 s 34) allows it
      `that court ordered no publication under s 35(2)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For the Supreme or District Court, whether it ordered that you not be identified
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May a report identifying me be published` IF
    CONSIDER `where`
    WHEN `in the Supreme Court or District Court, not on appeal from the Children's Court` THEN
        (NOT `that court ordered no publication under s 35(2)`) OR `the Supreme Court allowed it under s 36A or the PBO Act s 34 permits it`
    OTHERWISE `the Supreme Court allowed it under s 36A or the PBO Act s 34 permits it`

-- s 35(4): a contravention is punishable "(a) by the Supreme Court as for a contempt; or (b) after summary conviction by a
-- fine of $10 000 or imprisonment for 12 months." The same in s 36(2) for disclosure.
@export What fine can someone who unlawfully identifies me be given on summary conviction, in dollars (ss 35(4), 36(2))?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What fine can someone who identifies me be given` IS `the publication fine`

-- s 36A(3): "Only the Attorney General or the Commissioner of Police may make an application for an order under subsection
-- (1) and, unless the Court otherwise orders, the application shall be ex parte." (1): the Supreme Court decides "after
-- considering the public interest and the interests of the child".
DECLARE `Applicant` IS ONE OF
    `the Attorney General`
    `the Commissioner of Police`
    `a newspaper or broadcaster`
    `the victim`
    `me`

@export May this person ask the Supreme Court to allow my identification (s 36A(3))?
GIVEN `the applicant` IS A `Applicant` @desc Who wants to apply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May this person apply to allow my identification` IF
    CONSIDER `the applicant`
    WHEN `the Attorney General` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Commissioner of Police` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `36, 36AA -- may someone tell others how my case ended`

-- s 36(1): where "(a) a charge against a child is dismissed by the Court; or (b) a child is convicted or found guilty of an
-- offence by the Court, a person other than the child shall not, in a manner which identifies or is likely to lead to the
-- identification of the child, disclose the fact of the dismissal, the conviction or finding of guilt or any relevant order
-- except to a court of law, to a person acting in the performance of duties under any written law, to a person who as part
-- of the person's duties is concerned with the custody or welfare of the child, or in accordance with section 36AA(1) or an
-- order made under section 36A or in accordance with the Prohibited Behaviour Orders Act 2010 section 34."
-- s 36AA(1): disclosure "to a health department officer for use in the research project" ("Developmental Pathways Project").
-- The child may tell anyone: the ban is on "a person other than the child".
DECLARE `Recipient` IS ONE OF
    `a court of law`
    `a person performing duties under a written law`
    `a person whose duties concern my custody or welfare`
    `a health department officer for the Developmental Pathways Project`
    `an employer or anyone else`

@export May someone other than me tell this person, in a way that identifies me, how my Children's Court case ended (ss 36, 36AA)?
GIVEN `the recipient` IS A `Recipient` @desc Who would be told
      `the case ended in the Children's Court in a dismissal or a finding of guilt` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Children's Court dismissed the charge or found you guilty (or convicted you)
      `a Supreme Court order under s 36A or the PBO Act s 34 allows it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Supreme Court order under s 36A (or the Prohibited Behaviour Orders Act 2010 s 34) allows it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May someone else disclose how my case ended` IF
        (NOT `the case ended in the Children's Court in a dismissal or a finding of guilt`)
    OR  `a Supreme Court order under s 36A or the PBO Act s 34 allows it`
    OR  (    CONSIDER `the recipient`
             WHEN `an employer or anyone else` THEN FALSE
             OTHERWISE TRUE)

§§ `28, 40 -- can I have it looked at again`

-- s 40(1): where the Court "when constituted so as not to consist of or include a judge, makes a finding that a charge
-- against a child is proved and makes an order against or in relation to the child in consequence of that finding, the Court
-- when constituted by the President may ... reconsider the order". (2)(a): an application may be made "by the child, within
-- one month after the date of the order". (5): once "leave to appeal is granted under section 41 in respect of the original
-- order -- (a) no application under this section may subsequently be made". (2)(b): the CEO (young offenders) may apply on
-- the child's behalf "at any time".
@export Can I ask the President of the Children's Court to reconsider the order made against me (s 40)?
GIVEN `a judge sat` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court that made the order consisted of or included a judge
      `the date of the order` IS A DATE @desc The date the order was made
      `the date I apply` IS A DATE @desc The date you would apply
      `leave to appeal has been granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether leave to appeal against the same order has been granted under s 41
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I ask the President to reconsider my order` IF
        (NOT `a judge sat`)
    AND (NOT `leave to appeal has been granted`)
    AND Day `the date I apply` AT MOST Day (`one month after` `the date of the order`)

-- s 28(1): the Court may order that original proceedings in which an order "(not being an order made following conviction on
-- indictment) was made against a child under the Young Offenders Act 1994 or the Children and Community Services Act 2004
-- are to be reheard". (2): the order may be made by the Court constituted by the President "of the Court's own motion or upon
-- cause being shown on the application of ... (c) a child to whom the proceedings relate". (5): a rehearing is not itself
-- "original proceedings" for a further application.
@export May I apply for my case to be reheard (s 28)?
GIVEN `the order was made under the Young Offenders Act 1994 or the Children and Community Services Act 2004` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the order against you was made under one of those Acts
      `it followed a conviction on indictment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the order followed a conviction on indictment
      `the proceedings are themselves a rehearing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings were already a rehearing under s 28
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I apply for a rehearing` IF
        `the order was made under the Young Offenders Act 1994 or the Children and Community Services Act 2004`
    AND (NOT `it followed a conviction on indictment`)
    AND (NOT `the proceedings are themselves a rehearing`)
